WEBVTT

00:00.160 --> 00:03.930
- [Voiceover] So I have a
arbitrary inscribed quadrilateral

00:03.930 --> 00:06.740
in this circle and what I wanna prove

00:06.740 --> 00:09.960
is that for any inscribed quadrilateral,

00:09.960 --> 00:13.542
that opposite angles are supplementary.

00:13.542 --> 00:15.857
So when I say they're supplementary,

00:15.857 --> 00:18.080
the measure of this angle
plus the measure of this angle

00:18.080 --> 00:19.970
need to be 180 degrees.

00:19.970 --> 00:22.380
The measure of this angle
plus the measure of this angle

00:22.380 --> 00:24.412
need to be 180 degrees.

00:24.412 --> 00:26.650
And the way I'm gonna prove
it is we're gonna assume

00:26.650 --> 00:29.120
that this, the measure of
this angle right over here,

00:29.120 --> 00:31.461
that this is x degrees.

00:31.461 --> 00:33.410
And so from that, if we
can prove that the measure

00:33.410 --> 00:36.810
of this opposite angle
is 180 minus x degrees,

00:36.810 --> 00:38.970
then we've proven that opposite angles

00:38.970 --> 00:41.590
for an arbitrary quadrilateral
that's inscribed in a circle

00:41.590 --> 00:44.260
are supplementary, 'cause
if this is 180 minus x,

00:44.260 --> 00:48.380
180 minus x plus x is
going to be 180 degrees.

00:48.380 --> 00:50.610
So I encourage you to pause
the video and see if you can do

00:50.610 --> 00:53.110
that proof and I'll give
you a little bit of a hint.

00:53.110 --> 00:57.390
It's going to involve
the measure of the arcs

00:57.390 --> 01:00.246
that the various angles intercept.

01:00.246 --> 01:01.770
So let's think about it a little bit.

01:01.770 --> 01:06.770
This angle that has a
measure of x degrees,

01:06.770 --> 01:10.782
it intercepts this arc, so
we see one side of the angle

01:10.782 --> 01:13.070
goes and intercepts the circle there.

01:13.070 --> 01:15.630
The other side right over there.

01:15.630 --> 01:18.280
And so the arc that it intercepts,

01:18.280 --> 01:20.640
I am highlighting in yellow.

01:20.640 --> 01:23.818
I am highlighting it in yellow.

01:23.818 --> 01:26.490
Trying to color it in, so there you go.

01:26.490 --> 01:28.220
Not a great job at coloring it in,

01:28.220 --> 01:29.020
but you get the point.

01:29.020 --> 01:31.360
That's the arc that it intercepts

01:31.360 --> 01:34.150
and we've already learned
in previous videos

01:34.150 --> 01:39.150
that the relationship
between an inscribed angle,

01:39.150 --> 01:41.970
the vertex of this angle
sits on the circle,

01:41.970 --> 01:44.090
the relationship between
and inscribed angle

01:44.090 --> 01:47.460
and the measure of the
arc that it intercepts

01:47.460 --> 01:49.080
is that the measure of the inscribed angle

01:49.080 --> 01:52.200
is half the measure of the
arc that it intercepts.

01:52.200 --> 01:54.930
So if this angle measure is x degrees,

01:54.930 --> 01:58.521
then the measure of this
arc is going to be 2x,

01:58.521 --> 02:01.255
2x degrees.

02:01.255 --> 02:03.080
All right, well that's
kind of interesting,

02:03.080 --> 02:04.320
but let's keep going.

02:04.320 --> 02:06.600
If the measure of that arc is 2x degrees,

02:06.600 --> 02:10.410
what is the measure of
this arc right over here?

02:10.410 --> 02:12.727
The arc that completes the circle.

02:12.727 --> 02:14.370
Well, if you go all the
way around the circle,

02:14.370 --> 02:16.428
that's 360 degrees.

02:16.428 --> 02:19.710
So this blue arc that I'm
showing you right now,

02:19.710 --> 02:22.840
that's going to have a measure of 360

02:28.420 --> 02:31.011
minus 2x degrees.

02:31.011 --> 02:32.640
360's all the way around.

02:32.640 --> 02:36.900
The blue is all the way
around minus the yellow arc.

02:36.900 --> 02:39.360
What you have left over if you
subtract out the yellow arc

02:39.360 --> 02:41.483
is you have this blue arc.

02:41.483 --> 02:44.160
Now, what's the angle that intercepts

02:44.160 --> 02:45.693
this blue arc?

02:45.693 --> 02:47.530
What's the inscribed angle

02:47.530 --> 02:51.776
that intercepts this
blue arc right over here?

02:51.776 --> 02:53.399
Well, it's this angle.

02:53.399 --> 02:55.960
It's the angle that we
wanted to figure out

02:55.960 --> 02:57.280
in terms of x.

02:59.640 --> 03:01.730
Wow, I'm having trouble changing colors.

03:01.730 --> 03:04.960
It is that angle right over there.

03:04.960 --> 03:08.174
Notice, the two sides of this angle,

03:08.174 --> 03:11.842
they intercept, this
angle intercepts that arc.

03:11.842 --> 03:14.990
So, once again, the measure
of an inscribed angle

03:14.990 --> 03:17.060
is gonna half the measure of the arc

03:17.060 --> 03:18.500
that it intercepts.

03:18.500 --> 03:22.230
So what's 1/2, what is 1/2

03:22.230 --> 03:25.891
times 360 minus 2x?

03:25.891 --> 03:29.511
Well, one 1/2 times 360 is 180.

03:29.511 --> 03:32.607
1/2 times 2x is x.

03:32.607 --> 03:34.240
So the measure of this angle is gonna be

03:34.240 --> 03:36.774
180 minus x degrees.

03:36.774 --> 03:41.240
180 minus x degrees, and just like that

03:41.240 --> 03:43.170
we've proven that these opposite sides

03:43.170 --> 03:46.200
for this arbitrary
inscribed quadrilateral,

03:46.200 --> 03:47.650
that they are supplementary.

03:47.650 --> 03:50.420
You add these together,
x plus 180 minus x,

03:50.420 --> 03:52.000
you're going to get 180 degrees.

03:52.000 --> 03:54.150
So they are supplementary.
